Build pitch correctors from the metadata a play read, not a racing read

A run of the new scheduler differential test killed the test process
with SIGFPE. The cause is a race that can kill the daemon:

- A buffer still decoding answers SampleBuffer::channels() and
  sample_rate() with a non-blocking try_read, and returns 0 for both
  whenever its decoder holds the write lock to append a chunk.
- Signalsmith's presetDefault configures its STFT with
  blockSamples = 0.12 x rate; for rate 0 that is 0, and
  DynamicSTFT::moveOutput then computes `% 0` (stft.h:212), a hardware
  divide fault rather than a Rust panic.

Two routes reached it:

- A play starting with pitch correction (its own pitch_correction from
  0bf9fe5, or a pitch-corrected speed carried onto it while it loaded)
  went through set_speed_with_mode -> enable_pitch_correction ->
  PitchCorrector::new(channels(), sample_rate()). gdb on the new test:
  PitchCorrector::new(channels=0, sample_rate=0) <- enable_pitch_
  correction (mixer.rs:470) <- set_speed_with_mode <- handle_command.
- The Play arm recorded /status/samples sample_rate and channels with
  the same reads, and a later speed with pitch correction builds its
  bundle from that status. This route is on 2.1.0-rc.1: a cold play
  recorded rate 0 in 2-5% of plays under a decoder in these tests.

A play now records the channels and rate it read with
metadata_blocking() (already checked non-zero) in its status, and a
play starting with pitch correction gets its corrector the way a speed
command does: PitchBundle::for_voice from those values, installed with
apply_shipped_speed. That also gives it the scratch sized for a whole
block, so its first mixed block no longer grows pitch_scratch on the
audio thread.

enable_pitch_correction (still reached by the library-only
SetSpeedMatching) now refuses when the buffer cannot report its channels
or rate, and set_speed_with_mode then rejects the speed instead of
applying it without correction; the rest of the function uses the
values it checked.

docs/bugs.md records the same non-blocking rate read in SeekMatching,
which can send a seek on a still-decoding sound to frame 0. CHANGELOG
has a Fixed entry.

Tests (each failed first):
- a_play_with_pitch_correction_starts_while_its_decoder_writes_the_
  buffer: 100 pitch-corrected plays against a buffer whose stand-in
  decoder takes the write lock for 5 us out of every 25 us; SIGFPE 3 of
  3 runs before the change.
- a_play_records_its_rate_and_channels_while_its_decoder_writes_the_
  buffer: 300 plays; 6 to 15 statuses recorded (0, 0) per run before.
- a_play_with_pitch_correction_mixes_its_first_block_without_growing_
  scratch: 1 regrow before, 0 after.
- pitch_correction_is_refused_while_a_decoding_buffer_cannot_report_its_
  rate (mixer): SIGFPE before; now refused while the buffer is held and
  accepted once it is released.

Two adversarial reviewers found the same crash independently; their
traces match the one above.

Validation: cargo fmt --check, cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings,
and a warning-free release build (RUSTFLAGS=-D warnings); fuzz suite 7
passed; broker-backed native suite (MQTTAUDIO_BROKER_TESTS=1, TLS CA
set) 1027 passed, 0 failed, 7 device-gated ignored.

